today's classic interview is one that  many people in our audience just don't  know exists I get a lot of emails from  our audience saying David please  interview Matt Dillahunty and we have  interviewed Matt Dillahunty and that's  the interview we're going to look at  today Matt Dillahunty known as a debater  of religion taking the non-religious  perspective debunking lots of so-called  sacred texts and there are many people  in our audience who just don't know that  we've interviewed Matt Dillahunty  because they are relatively new  followers of the show so we will go to  my interview from February of 2014 with  Matt Dillahunty doesn't mean we won't  have him back on but this is a really  really interesting interview to check  out it talks to us about how he went  from almost being a Christian minister  to being a debunker of so-called sacred  texts let's check it out I'm joined  today by Matt Dillahunty he is host of  the Atheist Experience he's regularly  engaging in formal debates travels the  u.s. speaking to local secular  organizations and university groups as  part of the secular Student Alliance as  speakers bureau a bunch of our audience  actually suggested that we have Matt on  and interestingly enough Matt it it a  lot of those recommendations were after  I had a conversation with a pastor named  Carl Gallup's  and I had asked Carl to give me some  kind of evidence a reason why he  believes the Bible is the Word of God  that comes from outside the Bible itself  and we basically got to the point where  he was number one talking about  Deuteronomy as evidence from outside the  Bible  that the Bible is the Word of God but  then he also said that the fact that  things that the Bible predicts have  happened would be evidence that the  Bible is a Word of God so most of my  audience that's an argument that didn't  make sense I'm curious how you tackle  those arguments in the debates that you  do I think I would have done you know  very much the same thing you did I keep  asking for evidence from outside the  Bible so that we're not in this position  of constantly trying to verify because  the Bible's just claims it can't also be  the evidence for the claim  so I think I'd taken a similar task or  attacked on that but it's strange that  this is what keeps coming up I did a  debate last Friday with Ray Comfort  although I wouldn't call it a debate  where he just said flatly he had no  interest in demonstrating God existed we  already know were just lying about it  and he believes the Bible and that's all  there is to it so it's a very difficult  subject to get through because they're  beginning with a very different starting  point than we are about how you  determine what's reasonable to believe  when you were thinking of being a  Baptist minister for some time was there  a specific moment you remember where you  said not only am I not sure I want to be  a Baptist minister I don't think I  believe in Christianity anymore yeah  there was a I can't pinpoint a single  moment or a single event or even a  single argument I was basically studying  with the goal of eventually convincing  my atheist roommate although we had had  no conversations about this and this was  a fairly long period of study I was  between jobs as I am today  and over about 18 months or so it was  let me be the best defender of  Christianity I can be let me fulfill my  obligation under first Peter 3:15 to  give the reason for the faith that's  within me and at some point and I don't  remember exactly when or why it was just  like the the wealth of information just  kind of pushed my belief to collapse to  where I said you know I don't know what  I believe I don't know if I believe in a  God but I definitely don't believe what  I used to believe so let me investigate  further let me see if there's some other  God or some other God concept within  Christian or outside of it that could be  true or that I that I think is more  reasonable and that led to kind of a  deeper study of philosophy and logic and  skepticism to determine you know how to  direct that search so when that happened  was your initial feeling one of  disappointment or or being kind of an it  was it a negative emotional state or was  it in a way positive and liberating  because I could see it going either way  yeah and I have seen it go either way I  get tons of email from people who  are you know elated as I was you know by  the time I finally realized that I could  should identify as an atheist  it was incredibly liberating it was you  know it's not somebody I didn't dwell in  the fact that I had believed something  that I now thought was incorrect for so  many years I embraced the fact that now  I've been corrected it's it's kind of  the the foundation of science and and  similar thinking that you want to  believe as many true things and a few  false things as possible and so when  you're corrected that's a wonderful  thing because now you've got reality but  I've heard from so many people who were  just despondent and quite many of them  were upset over this idea that they had  lost an eternal life and that always  struck me as strange because I don't  couldn't see how you could lose  something that you never had that's  interesting and this idea of being open  to the changing of beliefs kind of came  up in this debate between Bill Nye and  Ken Ham from the creation is a museum I  have a couple of specific questions  about the debate for you but what were  your thoughts about the debate happening  in general it was very interesting to me  but my concern was that in some way it  was legitimizing the views of Ken Ham as  worthy of being debated in this very  kind of elaborate way with a scientist  and that that in and of itself was it  was a concern yeah that's a common  objection that people like Richard  Dawkins and others if it's the reason  why Richard doesn't debate creationist I  wasn't so much concerned for that reason  I was opposed to it in general because  my understanding is that the Creation  Museum was in financial trouble and to  me this was Bill Nye taking the  opportunity to make sure that they got a  bunch of money because no matter how  this debate turned out Ken Ham is going  to make money off this if you if he  would have done well in one which I  don't think is the case or even  portrayed it really well he that's a  marketing tool and if he fails as  evidently even Christian website polls  show that 92 percent thought bill bill  nye one that still becomes a marketing  tool for him because he can say look  we've got to do more we need more money  to debate this and do you know bring  more information and we've got to do a  better job all of this just kind of  funds the  Museum now overall I thought Bill did a  good job I mean there were mistakes but  Ken Ham was I'm sorry to say such a poor  debater that he just kind of missed them  one of the most interesting things  specifically from the debate was this  moment where they were each asked what  might change their minds and Ken Ham  essentially said nothing that it doesn't  matter what could what anybody could  tell him or show him he's going to  believe that the Bible is the little  literal word of God whereas Bill Nye  said well evidence would change my mind  and when people talk to me about this I  say I have no personal interest in there  not being a god I have no personal  interest in making those who believe  that feel bad or proving them wrong I  have an interest in just believing what  I think is the truth truth based on  evidence that has been presented to me  I'm 100% open to the idea that there is  a higher power of some kind it could be  a god it could be a more advanced  civilization that has created the  universe as a simulation as I've talked  to astro physicist Lawrence Krauss about  do you find that most non-believers are  in the camp of being open to their being  a God or they're in the camp of knowing  there is no God it's really hard to say  because I primarily you know run in  circles of skeptics and in this of those  atheists that are skeptics and that are  atheist because of skeptical reasons I  think that they're all open there were  hey should give us the evidence show us  you know we would like to believe as  many true things as few false things as  possible we're not beginning with this  we don't want to believe in a god thing  there are plenty of atheists out there  who just flatly assert no there's no  such thing as God and I have no interest  in you know that even possibly being  true and there are others who say things  similar that are often misrepresented  you know Hitchens famously said that you  know this idea of the Christian God in  particular he wouldn't even want it to  be true and what he was what he meant I  definitely agree with you know he was  talking about the character of this  individual in the model that they've  created in the universe for him but if  there is God I'd certainly like to know  about it I'm not in any way proposing  that it's necessarily impossible that  there's nothing that qualifies in that  realm it's just that I haven't seen the  evidence for it one of the things that  kind of bothers me sometimes when I hear  from people about kind of questioning my  my beliefs is the idea that what what if  I'm wrong type of thing and again it's  it's not one of these things where I'm  an econ any kind of crusade I I was  raised Jewish relatively secular I had a  couple years of Hebrew school it was  never understood within my family that  what I'm being taught is literally true  but the idea was there's kind of  interesting cultural stuff here about  Jews as a people that we kind of want  you to grow up in and it was never  adversarial for me in any way and I find  that that's sometimes something that  comes up very often talking to those who  are very religious that it seems it has  always been an adversarial thing for  them this does God exist or does God not  exist where for me it was hey I'm open  to it either way yeah it's not I don't  like you know I'd love to find out  whether or not this is actually true  there's the various claims that a God  exists I find that you know coming from  your background of primarily cultural  Judaism that yeah the conflict is not as  prevalent there in part cuz you have to  do so much work to become a Jew of you  if you weren't born one and the you know  Reform Judaism doesn't even require that  one actually believe in a God but coming  from my background there certainly is a  lot more adversarial mm-hmm  to this but my biggest I'm most  definitely an evangelical atheist or a  preaching atheist and actually I'm I'm  probably preaching on behalf of  skepticism but unlike some skeptics who  would say that they don't care what  people would believe they care about the  methods that they use to reach those  please  I actually also care about what people  believe because what people believe  informs their actions and their actions  have consequences for themselves and  others so I care that somebody you know  believes that their God has ordained for  example marriage as being one man one  woman forever the effects of that are  very real for people yeah it's the  actions they take on behalf of the  beliefs and it seems to me kind of  non-productive counterproductive to say  I don't care about your beliefs I only  care about your actions if you really  care about the actions you work to  educate people so that their beliefs  change we've been speaking with Matt  Dillahunty he is  host of the atheist experience check  that out Matt great to have you on and  I'm sure we had plenty of time for more  and we'll definitely have you back  thanks very much David I appreciate it

